   "source": {
    "image_url": "https://scontent-iad3-2.xx.fbcdn.net/v/t39.30808-6/875730128_122149883517348565_6157479691805632906_n.jpg?stp=dst-jpg_p720x720_tt6&_nc_cat=103&ccb=1-7&_nc_sid=127cfc&_nc_ohc=E4GgXkm3DI8Q7kNvwEcCvtE&_nc_oc=AdpGbaPb6DMLdF1vGppprdu2btv3hebGlOS3PzOb5YcYbnQIa0bal5sCH9_GZKbEiSg&_nc_zt=23&_nc_ht=scontent-iad3-2.xx&edm=ADqbNqUEAAAA&_nc_gid=-drO94B3fW3C7DEwFILS_w&_nc_tpa=Q5bMBQLPb-F-6VcAhF5yWiQOHG9NLnFdNqsb4MRO63kKmsWfoLuGisBgt-qssAvRC_ouJG78EznCMz_zYQ&oh=00_AQTITwaJYREtEhEFfxkjhw9K0hK5r9DSCVTnUunbZJFIqQ&oe=6AEFB016",
    "link": "https://www.facebook.com/share/p/1MU4612imD/",
    "origin": "manual",
    "page_id": "61590456964686",
    "text": "Everyone at the billionaire charity gala treated the mafia boss's autistic son like a disgrace, but I was the only waitress who dared to help him. The moment I knelt beside the terrified boy and tapped four times on the marble floor, his ruthless father ordered his men to investigate me. I didn't know that my kindness would soon make me a target, with strangers calling me a traitor.\n\nMy name is Sophie Vance, and that night, I was just another exhausted waitress at New York's luxurious Pierre Hotel.\n\nSix hours on my feet. A wine-stained uniform. Blisters burning inside my cheap black shoes.\n\nMy supervisor had warned us, \"Keep your eyes down, your mouths shut, and stay out of the guests' business.\"\n\nI needed that paycheck.\n\nThen I saw Dante Moretti.\n\nNew York's most feared mafia boss stood near the grand staircase, surrounded by men who looked ready to kill for him.\n\nBut my attention went straight to the little boy sitting alone behind him.\n\nHe looked about eight, wearing a miniature tuxedo. His hands covered his ears, his shoulders trembled, and his body rocked desperately.\n\nThe music was deafening.\n\nThe crowd was laughing.\n\nNobody noticed his distress.\n\nUntil a woman in a silver gown brushed against him.\n\nThe boy screamed.\n\n\"Good Lord!\" she snapped. \"Can't someone remove that embarrassing child?\"\n\nHer husband whispered, \"That's Moretti's son.\"\n\n\"Then money clearly can't buy decent parenting.\"\n\nAnother guest muttered, \"Imagine raising a defective heir.\"\n\nMy stomach twisted.\n\nBecause three years earlier, I'd watched my little brother Danny suffer the same way.\n\nOur father called him spoiled whenever noise overwhelmed him.\n\nBut I knew better.\n\nI'd sit beside Danny, tapping a gentle rhythm until he could breathe again.\n\nFour little taps.\n\nOur secret signal.\n\nDanny died three years ago.\n\nAnd I would have given anything to hear his laughter again.\n\nI set down my champagne tray.\n\n\"Don't!\" another waitress whispered. \"That's Moretti's kid!\"\n\nI kept walking.\n\nSuddenly, someone slammed into my shoulder.\n\nChampagne splashed across my shoes.\n\nGlass shattered.\n\nThe boy shrieked and curled into himself.\n\nI dropped to my knees, ignoring the broken shards.\n\nThen I tapped the marble.\n\nOnce.\n\nTwice.\n\nThree times.\n\nFour.\n\nThe boy's eyes flickered toward my hand.\n\nFor one fragile second, his rocking slowed.\n\nThen the entire ballroom fell silent.\n\nHeavy footsteps stopped behind me.\n\n\"Who gave you permission to approach my son?\"\n\nI looked up.\n\nDante Moretti towered over me, his expression colder than death.\n\nBefore I could answer, he turned toward his bodyguards.\n\n\"Find out everything about her.\"\n\nHis gaze returned to mine.\n\n\"And don't let her leave.\""